## Part 3: How Zero-Signal Nanobots Handle Cyber Attacks

A cyber attack requires three things: a target to attack, a channel to attack through, and a system that can change in response to the attack. Zero-signal nanobots lack all three.

| Cyber Attack Vector | Standard Nanobot | Zero-Signal Nanobot |
|---|---|---|
| **Network intrusion / port scanning** | Has a receiver. Has a network stack. Has open ports. | **Has no receiver. No network stack. No ports.** There is nothing to connect to. |
| **Malicious code injection** | Has writable memory. Accepts updates. | **Has no writable memory.** The program is a physical structure. You cannot "inject" code into a rock. |
| **Spoofed command** | Listens for commands. Can be fooled by a fake signal. | **Listens to nothing.** There is no command input. The bot's entire instruction set is internal and immutable. |
| **Exploit of OS vulnerabilities** | Runs an operating system with bugs. | **Has no operating system.** Runs a fixed physical state machine. No software means no software vulnerabilities. |
| **Cryptographic attack** | Has keys to steal, signatures to forge. | **Has no keys.** It doesn't authenticate anything. It doesn't trust anything external. It only trusts its own immutable memory compared to the physical environment. |
| **Supply chain attack (poisoned firmware)** | Firmware can be updated in the field. | The only "update" happens at the factory. If the factory is compromised, the bots are born wrong. But post-fabrication, **no update vector exists.** The attack surface closes at the moment of manufacture. |

**The Core Principle:** A zero-signal nanobot is a **closed system** in the thermodynamic sense. No information enters or leaves after fabrication. It has no API. No protocol. No handshake. You cannot hack a system that has no interface to the outside world except the physical environment it is designed to modify.

**The only "cyber attack" that works:** Compromise the factory where the immutable blueprints are etched. This is the single point of failure, and it is defended by physical, not cyber, security.

